ith minimal value is acceptable if it prevents the landlord from gaining free control.\n\nAlternatively, if you pass, and the landlord also passes, then your teammate keeps control — ideal! So passing allows your teammate to potentially continue playing (e.g., lead another card next round). This is cooperative play.\n\nIn expert Dou Dizhu, peasants often pass on teammate leads unless they can immediately end a chain or protect their partner. By passing, you signal trust and allow your teammate to keep leading — possibly setting up longer-term cooperation.\n\nGiven that:\n- Your teammate still has 14 cards — likely wants to lead.\n- You have limited high cards.\n- The landlord hasn’t shown aggression yet.\n\nPassing is safe and supportive.\n\nBut wait — can you pass? Yes. Rule: any player can pass at any time, even if they can beat the current play.\n\nTherefore, best move: **Pass**, to let your teammate maintain control of the play.\n\nEvaluation: Two promising options are playing ♥6 (minimal beat) or passing. Passing supports teamwork and gives initiative back to your teammate who may have a plan. Playing ♥6 takes over unnecessarily and wastes a card that could be used later in a sequence or combo.\n\nReason: To cooperate effectively, peasants should avoid taking tricks from each other unless strategically essential. Since the landlord has already passed, letting your teammate continue (by both peasants passing) allows them to lead a new trick immediately. This maximizes coordination and minimizes risk of wasting high cards.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
